---

A binary classifier that looks at a radiograph and decides whether a bone in it is
fractured. The interesting part is not the final model but the gap between it and
the baseline, which is the only way to know whether the sophistication paid for
itself.

## Two models, deliberately

The **baseline** does it the honest, old-fashioned way: load the images, preprocess
them, extract features by hand, and train a support vector machine. It reached
**58.6% accuracy** with an ROC AUC of **0.623** — better than a coin flip, and not
by enough to be worth anything clinically.

The **ResNet model** replaces the hand-built features with transfer learning from a
pre-trained network, fine-tuned on the fracture dataset. On the same 338-image test
set it reached **83.4% accuracy**, ROC AUC **0.834**.

That is a 25-point swing, and it is the result the project exists to produce.
Building the weak model first is what makes the strong one legible: without the
baseline, 83% is a number with nothing to compare it to.

Work was benchmarked against a published baseline from the imaging literature
([Int. J. Imaging Syst. Technol.](https://doi.org/10.1002/ima.22849)), so the target
was somebody else's reported result rather than a number I picked myself.

